{"term":"irony","senses":[{"id":"kaikki:04d185b5a12c0e0aae","definition":"The quality of a statement that, when taken in context, may actually mean something different from, or the opposite of, what is written literally; the use of words expressing something other than their literal intention, often in a humorous context.","example":"Irony, saying what it ne'er intends,\nCensures with praise, and speaks to foes as friends.","pos":"noun","synonyms":[],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"rhetoric","register":"countable, rhetoric, uncountable","wordFamily":["cosmic irony","dramatic irony","ironic","ironical","ironist","ironize","ironym","irony mark","postirony","post-irony","situational irony","Socratic irony"],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/irony#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:3b8de4c66b7e5f8412","definition":"Dramatic irony: a theatrical effect in which the meaning of a situation, or some incongruity in the plot, is understood by the audience, but not by the characters in the play.","example":"","pos":"noun","synonyms":[],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"","register":"countable, uncountable","wordFamily":["cosmic irony","dramatic irony","ironic","ironical","ironist","ironize","ironym","irony mark","postirony","post-irony","situational irony","Socratic irony"],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/irony#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:31d2010e4d92db987e","definition":"Socratic irony: ignorance feigned for the purpose of confounding or provoking an antagonist.","example":"","pos":"noun","synonyms":[],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"","register":"countable, uncountable","wordFamily":["cosmic irony","dramatic irony","ironic","ironical","ironist","ironize","ironym","irony mark","postirony","post-irony","situational